d school funds to provide substantial or physical rewards for top performers', 'Occasional delays  on salary differential, allowances and other additional compensation due to pending documents', '', 'Limited technical expertise among teachers/staff to identify structural integrity issues', 'Insufficient funds/budget to cover major physical repairs or construction of new building', 'Lack of school reading  teacher/school full time librarian', 'Unstable internet connection due to the school\'s geographical location', 'Desktops are not functional anymore, slow internet connection', '', '', '', '', '', '', '', '', '', '', 'Regular Conduct of anti-bullying awareness campaigns and seminars for pupils and parents./inclusion of the issues on every school PTA meetings and Homeroom meetings', 'Strict implementation of the DepEd Child Protection Policy (DO 40, s.2012)', 'Effective implementation of Project SARDO tracking system and home visitation programs by class advisers', '', '', '', '', 'Regular conduct of quarterly nationwide simultaneous earthquake and fire drills', 'Strong sense of \"bayanihan\" among community members during school activities and programs such as Brigada Eskwela and monthly Brigadahan', '', '', '', '', '', '', '', '', '', '', '', 'Regular conduct og mid-year performance review and coaching', 'strong leadership and collaborative effort of the SPT in hitting the KPIs', '', '', '', '', 'Transparent deliberation during the crafting of class program and teacher program', '', '', '', '', '', 'Regular tracking of expenditures', 'High level of transparency in financial reporting to the SPTA and school staff', 1, 5, 0, 1, 1, 6, 1, 0, 2, 4, 3, 1, 2, 0, 2, 5, 2, 0, 2, 2, 2, 1, 2, 2, 3, 2, 2, 2, 1, 2, 1, 2, 2, 3, 2, 5, 5, 2, 1, 3, 3, 3, 'strengthen home visits to encourage better attendance for struggling learners', 'Request additional digital tools from LGU or private partners to enhance ICT integration', 'The school currently has no registered School-Based ALS learners for this rating period', 'Attend workshops on digital designs for teacher-made materials', 'Implement programs/initiatives that primarily function advance learners  such as peer tutoring ', 'Celebrate monthly themes tied to peace and core values./ Initiate activities to promote peace and DepEd core values.', 'Provide mandatory post-exam SLAC session to discuss least mastered competencies.', 'No TVL courses offered since this is an elementary institution.\r\n', 'Strengthen programs on anti-bullying and increase presence of faculty during recess and dismissal time for monitoring.', 'Conduct quarterly orientation for all stakeholders on Child Protection and Positive Discipline', 'Links some NGO partners for educational support; institutionalize a flexible learning option for pupils in crisis (ADM)', 'Organize a cultural exchange day where pupils showcase local traditions and crafts', 'Launch an advocacy campaign specifically targeting displaced or disadvantaged youth in the community', 'No  School-Based ALS program implemented', 'Update Child Protection Policy manual and Display Child Protection Flowchart in visible areas around the campus./Functional teen center', 'Conduct a Hazzard Mapping activity with student leaders and allocate a portion of the MOOE for emergency equipment maintenance', 'Utilize social media or group chats to keep parents informed and involved', 'No Special Education program implemented', 'Conduct a mid-year SIP review with the school planning team to ensure targets are still relevant and being met', 'Formalize the roles of each community member in the planning team trough memos/letters to increase accountability and engagement', 'Organize a \"Leadership and Management Workshop\" for newly elected student officers to build their capacity for independent project implementation', 'Develop a simplified feedback system to easily track stakeholdrr satisfaction and identify areas for service improvement', 'Conduct regular  monthly faculty meetings and include reviewing the AIP progress and adjusting timeline as needed.', 'Reorganize the SGC by conducting a formal election of officers and holding an orienattion session on the SGCs roles in school policy and decision-making.', 'Implement a transparent physical bulletin board for PTA financial reports and project updates to build higher trust and participation among members', 'Establish a stakeholder directory to formalize and track partnership', 'Ensure that M&E results are discussed duringb SLAC sessions to inform future planning', 'Provide a digital portfolio management system to help teachers organize MOVs through out the year', 'Strengthen the internal audit and monitoring of the AIP to ensure 100% completion of programs ', 'Standardized the monthly LAC plan based on the actual self-assessment (e-SAT) results of teachers', 'Create a school-based \"Professional Growth Tracking System\"', 'Conduct monthly or quarterly teacher service recognition based on clear criteria', 'Establish a school-based Allowances/Compensation Tracker to ensure zero delay in paperwork', 'Conduct an annual Workload Audit', 'Collaborate with the municipal engineers\' office for a comprehensive annual safety inspection of all school building', 'Prepare an activity proposal to be submitted for LGU funding', 'Create a functional classroom reading  corner to supplement the school reading center', 'Look for more high-gain satellite internet service provider', 'Submit a proposal for DepEd Computerization Program if available', 'Maintain a procurement calendar to ensure early purchase of supplies/materials', 'Conduct a monthly liquidation pre-audit at school level', 'Distribute simplified satisfaction survey during HRPTA meetings and SPTA general assemblies', 1, 149, 12, 59);
INSERT INTO `sbm_ta` (`id`, `fy`, `school_id`, `q1`, `q2`, `q3`, `q4`, `q5`, `q6`, `q7`, `q8`, `q9`, `q10`, `q11`, `q12`, `q13`, `q14`, `q15`, `q16`, `q17`, `q18`, `q19`, `q20`, `q21`, `q22`, `q23`, `q24`, `q25`, `q26`, `q27`, `q28`, `q29`, `q30`, `q31`, `q32`, `q33`, `q34`, `q35`, `q36`, `q37`, `q38`, `q39`, `q40`, `q41`, `q42`, `qq1`, `qq2`, `qq3`, `qq4`, `qq5`, `qq6`, `qq7`, `qq8`, `qq9`, `qq10`, `qq11`, `qq12`, `qq13`, `qq14`, `qq15`, `qq16`, `qq17`, `qq18`, `qq19`, `qq20`, `qq21`, `qq22`, `qq23`, `qq24`, `qq25`, `qq26`, `qq27`, `qq28`, `qq29`, `qq30`, `qq31`, `qq32`, `qq33`, `qq34`, `qq35`, `qq36`, `qq37`, `qq38`, `qq39`, `qq40`, `qq41`, `qq42`, `a1`, `a2`, `a3`, `a4`, `a5`, `a6`, `a7`, `a8`, `a9`, `a10`, `a11`, `a12`, `a13`, `a14`, `a15`, `a16`, `a17`, `a18`, `a19`, `a20`, `a21`, `a22`, `a23`, `a24`, `a25`, `a26`, `a27`, `a28`, `a29`, `a30`, `a31`, `a32`, `a33`, `a34`, `a35`, `a36`, `a37`, `a38`, `a39`, `a40`, `a41`, `a42`, `f1`, `f2`, `f3`, `f4`, `f5`, `f6`, `f7`, `f8`, `f9`, `f10`, `f11`, `f12`, `f13`, `f14`, `f15`, `f16`, `f17`, `f18`, `f19`, `f20`, `f21`, `f22`, `f23`, `f24`, `f25`, `f26`, `f27`, `f29`, `f30`, `f31`, `f32`, `f33`, `f34`, `f35`, `f36`, `f37`, `f38`, `f39`, `f40`, `f41`, `f42`, `f28`, `stat`, `district`, `region`, `division`) VALUES
(700, '2026', '128509', '1. Foundational Skills Gaps *Learners struggle with basic reading skills (phonics, decoding, fluency)\r\n*Limited vocabulary and difficulty in comprehension\r\n*Weak number sense (counting, place value, basic operations)\r\n*Difficulty applying skills to real-life contexts.       2. Learning Readiness and Diversity Issues *Presence of slow readers in Grade *Varied learning levels.              3. Teaching and Instructional Gaps\r\n*Limited use of differentiated instruction.*Lack of engaging and interactive learning strategies.4.Home and Parental Support Gaps\r\n*Limited parental involvement in reading and numeracy practice. *Lack of guidance for parents on how to support learning at home\r\n*Socio-economic challenges affecting learning time and resources.', '1.Foundational Skill Gaps\r\n*Weak reading comprehension (difficulty interpreting complex texts, especially in English).\r\n*Poor numeracy skills (struggles with problem-solving and multi-step math tasks).\r\n2.Higher-Order Thinking Deficits\r\n*Difficulty with critical thinking, analysis, and evaluation.\r\n*Struggles in applying concepts to real-world contexts (application-based NAT items)\r\nstanding. 3.21st Century Skills Deficiency\r\n*Limited digital literacy.\r\n*Weak collaboration and communication skills. 4.Motivation and Attitude Issues   *Poor study habits and time management. 4.Teaching Strategies Issues *Limited use of differentiated instruction for diverse learners.\r\n*Insufficient practice on higher-order thinking questions (HOTS).ICT and 5.Learning Resources\r\n*Lack of computers, and digital tools.\r\n*Lack of updated textbooks and learning materials. 6.Home Support Issues\r\n*Limited parental guidance or academic support.\r\n*Parents’ low educational background affecting assistance.', 'The school has no data regarding this indicator because the school has no ALS enrollees that\'s why there is no ALS  class/  ALS  program offered.', '1. Teacher Competency Gaps Issue:\r\n*Limited skills in contextualization and localization of content. 2.Resource and Technology Limitation Issues :\r\n*Lack of computers.\r\n*Inadequate access to updated references, books, or multimedia content. 3.External and Environmental Constraints Issue:\r\n*Limited parental/community support in providing contextual data.', '', '', '1. Technical Knowledge and Skills Gaps\r\nIssues:\r\n*Limited understanding of item analysis concepts (difficulty index, discrimination index, distractor efficiency)\r\n*Difficulty interpreting TIA results and translating them into instructional actions\r\n*Lack of skills in using statistical tools or software (e.g., Excel)  2. Time Constraints and Workload\r\nIssues:\r\n*Limited time to conduct thorough item analysis after assessments.                    3. Data Utilization Gaps\r\nIssues:\r\n*Results of item analysis are not maximized for instructional improvement.\r\n*Lack of action plans based on findings.    4.Collaboration and Professional Support Issues\r\n*Limited discussion of TIA results during LAC sessions.\r\n*Lack of peer support in analyzing and interpreting data.              5. Monitoring and Feedback Gaps\r\nIssues:\r\n*Lack of feedback on the quality of item analysis and test construction. 6.Attitude and Practice Issues\r\n*Perception that TIA is an additional burden rather than a useful tool.\r\n', 'Not applicable for an elementary school.', '', '', '', '1. Limited Cultural Awareness and Training\r\n*Teachers and staff lack sufficient knowledge or training on culturally responsive practices. 2.Limited Student Participation\r\n*Some students may feel shy, excluded, or uncomfortable participating due to cultural differences 3.Budget Constraints\r\n*Insufficient funding can limit the scope, quality, and sustainability of cultural activities.', '1.Stigma and Low Self-Confidence Among Learners\r\n*OSYs and adult learners may feel embarrassed returning to school or fear judgment. 2.Time Constraints and Scheduling Conflicts\r\n*Rigid schedules may not suit working learners or those with family responsibilities.            3.Weak Stakeholder and Community Support\r\n*Limited support community leaders reduces outreach and sustainability.', 'Lack of learner enrollment.', '', '', '1.Stigma and reluctance to seek help\r\n*Mental health concerns are often avoided due to fear, embarrassment, or cultural attitudes, resulting in underutilization of services. 2.Limited trained personnel\r\n*There are  no trained guidance counselors or staff equipped to handle mental health concerns effectively.', '1. Absence of accessible infrastructure\r\n*No accessible toilets, no standard ramps/pathways for learners with disabilities. 2. Lack of SPED-specific facilities and learning spaces\r\n*No dedicated rooms or resources tailored for learners with special educational needs.                3. Lack of trained personnel to support SPED learners\r\nNo teachers trained in handling learners with special needs. 4.Insufficient funding for facility improvement\r\n*Limited or no allocated budget for constructing or upgrading PWD-friendly infrastructure.', '1.Incomplete data like NAT results. 2.Low  participation of community partners. 3.Scarcity  of funds to support learners involvement to various activities  outside the school and for the development school projects .', '1.Inconsistent attendance of SCPT members during meetings. 2. Time constraints due to members\' multiple responsibilities.           3. Lack of funds for  program/ project implementation.         4. Lack of facilities and ICT support. ', '1.Representation Gaps  *Popularity vs. capability *Elections often favor well-known students rather than those with strong leadership skills. 2.Communication Breakdown         *Weak feedback channels between students and SSG/SPG officers. 3.Leadership and Skills Gaps\r\n*Officers  lack of training.  4.Participation Apathy  *Low attendance in activities unless graded or required.', '1.Lack of customer service training for frontline personnel. 2.Poor record-keeping systems (risk of lost or incomplete records). 3.No backup systems for important student data.', '1. Inconsistent monitoring and follow-through\r\nImplementation plans are carried out, but tracking of progress and completion is not done regularly, leading to uneven results.  2.Limited alignment between plans and actual practice\r\nSome activities in the implementation plan are not fully executed as designed due to shifting priorities. 3.Resource constraints\r\nBudget, materials, or manpower are sometimes inadequate, causing delays or partial implementation. 4.External constraints and interruptions\r\n*Unforeseen events (e.g., schedule disruptions/suspension of classes, community issues) affect the continuity of implementation.', '1. Irregular or absent SGC meetings\r\n*Meetings are seldom conducted or not scheduled consistently, limiting decision-making and coordination.\r\n2. Lack of clear roles and responsibilities\r\n*SGC members are not fully aware of their functions, leading to low accountability and inactive participation.\r\n3. Weak stakeholder engagement\r\n*Parents, community members, and partners have minimal involvement or interest in SGC activities.\r\n4. Limited awareness of SGC purpose and importance.\r\n*Stakeholders do not fully understand the role of the SGC in school governance and improvement.', '1.Inconsistent attendance and participation of parents\r\n*Some parents attend meetings and activities, but participation is not regular or sustained. 2.Scheduling conflicts among members\r\n*Work and personal commitments of parents affect their availability for meetings and activities.            3. Uneven engagement of PTA officers and members\r\n*Some officers are active, while others show limited participation or responsibility.            4.Resource limitations for PTA activities\r\n*Funds and materials are sometimes insufficient to fully support planned PTA programs. 5. Limited training or orientation for PTA officers\r\n*Some members lack understanding of their roles and responsibilities, affecting effectiveness.', '', '1. Data Collection & Management\r\n*Incomplete, inconsistent, or delayed submission of reports. 2. Capacity & Skills of Personnel\r\n*Limited training in monitoring and evaluation (M&E)processes.\r\n*Lack of data analysis and interpretation skills.          3. Time Constraints & Workload\r\n*Teachers and staff overloaded with multiple responsibilities.                     4. Communication & Coordination              *Minimal stakeholder involvement (parents, students, community)5.Resource Constraints\r\n*Insufficient budget for the implementation and  monitoring activities .', '1.Communication & Transparency *Important announcements not reaching all stakeholders on time.                  2. Stakeholder Engagement                    *Weak partnerships with community, alumni, or local organizations', '', '', '', 'Limited Training Opportunities, •	Limited funds for professional development programs , ', '', '', '', '', '1.Funding Instability and Limitations\r\n*Although improvements are frequent, funds are not enough to cover all priority needs.\r\n*Budget is often consumed by emergency repairs, leaving little for long-term upgrades.2.Stakeholder Engagement Issues *Low sustained involvement of parents and community in maintenance efforts.', 'Funding Constraints\r\n*No allocated budget for library establishment and development..', '1.Intermittent Utility Supply\r\n*Unstable water supply (low pressure).2.Aging or Inadequate Infrastructure\r\n*Old or poorly maintained electrical wirings.    3. Budget Constraints for classroom rewiring and improvement of water supply..\r\n', '1. Absence of ICT Infrastructure\r\n*No computer laboratory or ICT room for learners. *Lack of essential equipment such as desktop computers, laptops, printers, and networking devices.', '', '', '', '', '', '', '1.Strong Teacher Commitment and Competence\r\n*Teachers demonstrate high level of dedication to improving learner outcomes.\r\n*Continuous professional development through LAC sessions and trainings.                   2. Effective Use of Assessment  *Administration of Pre-tests / diagnostic tests and \r\nFormative assessments.\r\n*Use of results to group learners based on needs.\r\n*Data-driven planning of remediation sessions.3.Availability of Remediation Programs and Materials\r\n*Presence of structured programs such as:\r\nReading intervention programs\r\nNumeracy enhancement activities\r\n*Availability of:\r\nSelf-learning modules (SLMs)\r\nand activity sheets.\r\n*Contextualized remediation materials.                   4. Allocated Time for Remediation\r\n*Scheduled remediation periods within class hours or after class.           5.Learner Grouping and Differentiated Instruction\r\n*Use of ability grouping (fast, average, struggling learners).              *Peer tutoring and collaborative learning strategies.                  6.Strong School Leadership and Support\r\n*School heads prioritize learning recovery programs.\r\n*Monitoring and feedback on remediation implementation.\r\nProvision of technical assistance and instructional supervision.                7.Collaboration Among Teachers\r\n*Sharing of remediation strategies and materials during LAC sessions.\r\n*Team planning for intervention programs.  8.Monitoring and Feedback Mechanisms\r\n*Regular tracking of learner progress.\r\n*Immediate feedback to learners.\r\n*Adjustment of strategies based on improvement data', '1. Strong Teacher Values Orientation and Commitment\r\n*Teachers consistently demonstrate understanding and modeling of the DepEd Core Values: Maka-Diyos, Maka-tao, Makakalikasan, Makabansa.\r\n*Teachers serve as role models of respectful and ethical behavior.                     2. Integration of Values in Lesson Planning\r\n*Lesson plans explicitly include peace education and core values integration.\r\n*Inclusion of values in:\r\nLearning activities\r\nDiscussion questions\r\nPerformance tasks    3. Effective Classroom Management Practices\r\n*Use of positive discipline strategies.\r\n*Establishment of respectful, inclusive, and safe learning environments. 4.Use of Contextualized and Relevant Materials\r\n*Integration of local culture and real-life experiences in lessons.5. Supportive School Culture and Leadership\r\n*School heads encourage integration of values in all subject areas and \r\nimplementation of school-wide programs such as:\r\nPeace Month activities\r\nFlag ceremonies emphasizing values formation.                6. Collaboration Among Teachers\r\n*Sharing of values-integrated lesson exemplars during LAC sessions.\r\n*Joint planning of activities promoting peace education.           7. Community and Stakeholder Support\r\n*Participation of parents and community in values formation activities.                  8. Monitoring and Reinforcement Mechanisms\r\n*Regular classroom observation focusing on values integration.\r\n*Recognition of teachers who effectively integrate values education.', '', '', '1. Strong Implementation of Child Protection Policy (CPP). *Presence of a functional Child Protection Committee (CPC). 2. Teacher Awareness and Proactive Classroom Management *Immediate intervention when minor conflicts arise.           3. Values Education and Peace Promotion\r\nIntegration of respect, empathy, and kindness in daily lessons. 4.Awareness Campaigns and School Programs\r\nConduct\r\nChild protection  and anti-bullying orientations and\r\nSchool assemblies promoting discipline and respect. 5.Active Parent and Community Involvement\r\n*Parents are oriented on child protection and anti-bullying policies.\r\n*Coordination between school and community stakeholders.        6.Monitoring and Reporting System\r\n*Regular monitoring of student behavior and school climate.\r\n*Immediate documentation and resolution of any reported concern.', '1. Strong Implementation of Child Protection Policy (CPP)\r\n*Presence of an active and functional Child Protection Committee (CPC), *Strict adherence to DepEd Child Protection Policy guidelines.                     2.Preventive Education and Values Formation\r\n*Integration of child rights, personal safety, and respect in lessons\r\n*Promotion of values such as empathy, discipline, and self-respect .3.. Safe and Supportive School Environment\r\n*Establishment of a child-friendly, inclusive, and respectful school climate. 4. Active Parent and Community Involvement\r\n*Parents are oriented on  Child protection policies and\r\nPositive parenting practices.\r\n*Strong coordination with\r\nBarangay officials,\r\nSocial workers and\r\nCommunity stakeholders.       5.Effective Monitoring and Reporting Mechanisms\r\n*Functional and confidential reporting systems.\r\n*Immediate response to any reported or suspected case.           6. Continuous Monitoring, Evaluation, and Improvement\r\n*Review of child protection programs and policies.\r\n*Documentation and analysis of school safety data.\r\n', '1. Strong student monitoring system\r\n*The school consistently tracks attendance, performance, and behavior, allowing early identification of at-risk students and immediate intervention.  2.Positive and safe school environment\r\n*The school maintains a welcoming, respectful, and bullying-free atmosphere where students feel secure and valued. 3. Active parental and community involvement\r\n*Parents and community stakeholders are consistently engaged through meetings,programs, and communication, reinforcing the importance of education.  4.Flexible learning options\r\n*Alternative delivery modes (e.g., modular or blended learning, ) are offered for students with special circumstances. 5.Recognition and motivation systems\r\n*The school regularly acknowledges student achievements and improvements, boosting confidence and persistence.', '', '', '', '1.Designated CPC members with specific tasks \r\n2.Orientation of members of their roles and responsibilities to ensure accountability. 3.Teachers, parents, students, and community partners are engaged and aware of child protection initiatives.  4.Time, budget, and materials are allocated to sustain the committee’s programs and activities.', '1.Established DRRM committee with clear roles\r\n*A well-organized DRRM team is in place, with defined responsibilities for preparedness, response, and recovery.2.Conduct of regular drills and simulations\r\n*Earthquake, fire, and evacuation drills are consistently practiced and evaluated for improvement. 3.Integration into school programs and curriculum\r\nDRRM concepts are embedded in teaching, school activities, and awareness campaigns. 4Capacity building and training\r\n*Teachers, staff, and students are trained on  .disaster preparedness, first aid, and emergency protocols.             5.Strong coordination with local authorities and partners\r\n*Linkages with local DRRM offices, health units, and emergency responders are active and functional.', '', '', '', '', '', '', '', '', '', '1.Regular coordination and communication\r\n*The school maintains continuous communication through meetings, consultations, and digital platforms to strengthen collaboration.. 2.Culture of collaboration and openness\r\n*The school promotes teamwork, trust, and openness, encouraging continuous engagement with external partners.3.Continuous monitoring and evaluation of partnerships\r\n*Partnership activities are regularly reviewed to assess effectiveness and improve future collaboration.\r\n4.Recognition and sustainability of partnerships\r\n*Successful partnerships are acknowledged and sustained through giving of certificates of  appreciation of partner contributions.', '', '', 'Adequate resources and support, Regular monitoring and feedback, •	Constructive feedback helps improve performance\r\nProfessional development opportunities\r\n', 'Strong instructional leadership, Systematic needs assessment, Collaborative and supportive culture', 'Strong Instructional Leadership , Active Teacher Participation and Engagement , Data-Driven Decision Making ', '', 'Established Recognition and Reward System, Active Monitoring of Personnel Achievements', 'Competent and Trained Administrative Staff, Competent and Trained Administrative Staff, ', 'Clear Policies and Guidelines on Workload Allocation, Data-Driven Planning and Scheduling, Collaborative Decision-Making Process', '1.Use of Standardized Tools\r\n*Availability of inspection checklists and reporting forms.\r\n*Use of digital tools or logs for tracking  and reporting.\r\n*Organized documentation system (records, photos, reports) 2. Responsive Maintenance System\r\n*Clear reporting and feedback mechanism for defects  .3.Adequate Resources and Budget\r\n*Allocated funds for maintenance and minor repairs .4.Stakeholder Participation\r\n*Teachers and students encouraged to report hazards or damages.\r\n*Parents and community involved in school maintenance programs 5.Leadership and Accountability\r\n*Active involvement of school head in monitoring.\r\n*Assigned physical facilities  coordinator.\r\n\r\n', '', '', '', '', '1.Strong Financial Planning and Budgeting\r\n*Clear Annual Implementation Plan (AIP) aligned with the School Improvement Plan (SIP).\r\n*Prioritization of expenses based on urgency, relevance, and impact on learning.                  2. Efficient Procurement and Fund Utilization System\r\n*Timely and compliant procurement processes following government guidelines.                       3. Transparent Financial Management\r\n*Regular financial reporting and monitoring of MOOE utilization.\r\n*Clear documentation of expenditures (receipts, liquidation reports, summaries).              4. Needs-Based Prioritization System\r\n*Allocation of funds based on actual school needs (maintenance, utilities, learning resources).', '1.Compliance with Government Rules and Regulations\r\n*Strict adherence to COA and DepEd financial guidelines and deadlines. 2.Organizational Discipline and Culture of Compliance\r\n*Strong culture of transparency, accountability, and timely submission of requirements.\r\n*Awareness among staff that liquidation is a mandatory and non-negotiable process.. 3.Timely and Complete Documentation\r\n*Availability of complete supporting documents (receipts, invoices, vouchers, liquidation reports).', 1, 1, 2, 1, 1, 1, 1, 2, 6, 6, 6, 2, 6, 6, 2, 2, 6, 5, 1, 1, 1, 1, 2, 2, 6, 2, 2, 2, 1, 1, 1, 3, 1, 1, 1, 2, 3, 3, 1, 5, 3, 2, '1. Strengthening Foundational Skills Development\r\n*Implement structured reading programs focusing on phonics, decoding, fluency, and comprehension. *Use contextualized and real-life applications of numeracy and literacy to deepen understanding;\r\n*Provide remediation programs for struggling learners. 2. Addressing Learning Readiness and Diversity\r\n*Conduct baseline and diagnostic assessments to identify learner levels.                    *Develop Individual Learning Plans (ILPs) for slow readers and learners needing support.\r\n3. Enhancing Teaching and Instructional Practices  *Integrate interactive and learner-centered strategies such as games, storytelling, and hands-on activities.               *Utilize instructional materials and visual aids to support varied learning styles.                                  4. Strengthening Home–School Partnership  *Conduct parent orientation and workshops on supporting reading and numeracy at home.                *Establish regular communication (meetings, messages, updates) between teachers and parents.                    5. Addressing Socio-Economic Barriers\r\n*Provide learning resources (books, worksheets, kits) for learners with limited access.\r\n*Coordinate with stakeholders and partners for resource support and assistance programs.                         6. Monitoring and Evaluation  *Monitor effectiveness of interventions and adjust strategies accordingly.\r\n*Report progress to school leaders and parents to ensure transparency and accountability.', 'Foundational Skill Gaps:                         1.Weak reading comprehension Proposed Resolutions:\r\n* Reading Intervention Programs like   implement daily structured reading sessions (e.g., DEAR – Drop Everything and Read),\r\nuse leveled reading materials and contextualized texts. and conduct reading diagnostics and group learners based on ability       2.Poor numeracy skills                *Numeracy  Enhancement such as conducting  daily numeracy drills (basic operations to problem-solving),\r\nusing contextualized math problems (real-life applications) and \r\nproviding remediation classes for struggling learners.                             2. Higher-Order Thinking Deficits Issues:\r\n*Weak critical thinking, analysis, evaluation\r\n*Difficulty applying concepts\r\nProposed Resolutions:\r\na. Integration of HOTS in Daily Lessons\r\n*Design lesson plans with Bloom’s Taxonomy levels (analyze, evaluate, create).                         b. Problem-Based and Inquiry-Based Learning\r\n*Implement real-life problem-solving tasks.                                    c. NAT-Oriented Practice\r\n*Provide regular exposure to NAT-type questions.\r\nConduct  monthly mock tests.                       3. 21st Century Skills Deficiency\r\n Issues:\r\n*Limited digital literacy\r\n*Weak collaboration and communication Proposed Resolutions:\r\na. ICT Integration in Teaching\r\n*Use available tools (even limited) like:\r\nMobile phones for research.\r\n*Offline digital resources (videos, modules).                           b. Collaborative Learning Strategies\r\n*Implement group work, peer discussions, and projects.                         c. Communication Skill Development\r\n*Include oral presentations and reporting activities.\r\n*Encourage use of English and Filipino in academic discussions                 .4. Motivation and Attitude Issues\r\n Issues:\r\n*Poor study habits\r\n*Weak time management Proposed Resolutions:\r\na. Study Skills Training\r\n*Conduct sessions on Time management strategies and \r\nGoal setting\r\nb. Learner Monitoring System\r\n*Track academic progress every quarter.\r\n*Provide feedback and recognition for improvements.          c. Incentive and Recognition Programs\r\n*Give rewards for:\r\nImproved performance.\r\nActive participation.\r\n5. Teaching Strategies Issues\r\nIssues:\r\n*Limited differentiated instruction\r\n*Insufficient HOTS practice\r\nProposed Resolutions:\r\na. Differentiated Instruction\r\n*Group learners based on ability levels\r\nProvide:\r\nSimplified tasks for struggling learners\r\nEnrichment tasks for advanced learners. b. Capacity Building for Teachers\r\n*Conduct LAC sessions and trainings on:\r\nDifferentiated teaching and \r\nHOTS integration\r\nAssessment design\r\nc. Use of Formative Assessment\r\n*Frequent quizzes and feedback mechanisms.\r\n*Adjust teaching based on learner performance.           6. ICT and Learning Resources Constraints\r\nIssues:\r\n*Lack of computers and digital tools\r\n*Lack of updated materials\r\nProposed Resolutions:\r\na. Resource Maximization\r\n*Use low-cost and improvised materials.                         b. School-Based Resource Development\r\n*Teachers create localized and contextualized materials.                           c. Partnerships and Stakeholder Support\r\n*Collaborate with:\r\nLGUs\r\nNGOs\r\nPrivate donors\r\nfor ICT donations and learning materials.                        7. Home Support Issues:\r\n*Limited parental support\r\n*Low educational background of parents\r\nProposed Resolutions:\r\na. Parent Engagement Programs\r\n*.Conduct Parent Orientation on NAT and learner support\r\n*Provide simple guides on how parents can help at home.                 c. *Regular Communication\r\nUse:\r\n*Text messaging\r\n*Group chats\r\n*Parent-teacher conferences\r\n', '1. Community Mapping and Learner Identification\r\n*Conduct a simple survey or mapping in nearby barangays to identify:\r\nOut-of-school youth (OSY)\r\nAdults without elementary or JHS completion\r\n*Coordinate with:\r\nBarangay officials\r\nDay care centers\r\nLocal youth organizations                 2. Advocacy and Awareness Campaign\r\n*Many potential learners don’t enroll because they’re  hesitant.\r\nActions:\r\n*Orientation sessions in the community\r\nPosting of announcements in tarp/ social media .\r\n', '1. Teacher Competency Gaps\r\nIssue:\r\n*Limited skills in contextualization and localization of content\r\n Proposed Resolutions:\r\na. Capacity-Building and Training Programs\r\n*Conduct regular LAC (Learning Action Cell) sessions focused on\r\nContextualization and localization strategies.                        b. Mentoring and Coaching System\r\n*Pair less experienced teachers with master teachers or subject experts.      2. Resource and Technology Limitations\r\nIssues:\r\n*Lack of computers.\r\n*Inadequate access to updated references, books, or multimedia content.\r\n Proposed Resolutions:\r\na. Resource Maximization and Innovation\r\n*Encourage use of\r\nMobile phones for research and content creation. *Promote improvised and low-cost instructional materials using locally available resources.                         b. Establishment of a Learning Resource Bank\r\n*Develop a shared repository (printed and digital) of:\r\nContextualized lesson plans,\r\nactivity sheets and \r\nmultimedia resources.                          c. Strengthening Partnerships\r\n*Collaborate with:\r\nLGUs (Local Government Units),\r\nPTA (Parents-Teachers Association),\r\nNGOs and private stakeholders\r\nfor donations of\r\nComputers/laptops.3. External and Environmental Constraints\r\nIssue:\r\n*Limited parental/community support in providing contextual data\r\nProposed Resolutions:\r\na. Strengthening Parent Engagement\r\n*Conduct parent orientations on the importance of contextualized learning.\r\n*Provide simple guides on how parents can contribute (e.g., sharing local practices, stories, data).                              b. School-Community Partnerships\r\n*Collaborate with:\r\nBarangay officials and \r\nLocal experts (farmers, artisans, health workers)\r\nas resource persons for authentic content.\r\nc. Feedback and Communication Mechanisms\r\n*Maintain regular communication through Group chats and \r\nMeetings.', 'PROPOSED COMMITMENTS\r\n1. Strong Teacher Commitment and Competence:\r\n*Teachers commit to continuously enhancing their instructional and remediation skills through active participation in LAC sessions, INSET, and relevant trainings. 2. Effective Use of Assessment\r\nCommitment:\r\n*Teachers commit to regularly administering diagnostic and formative assessments to identify learner needs.\r\n*Teachers commit in using assessment data to plan, implement, and adjust remediation strategies based on learner performance.                  3. Availability of Remediation Programs and Materials\r\nCommitment:\r\n*Teachers commit to developing, utilizing, and improving contextualized remediation materials aligned with learner needs. &Teachers commit to maximizing available learning resources (SLMs, activity sheets, and localized materials) to support remediation.                    4. Allocated Time for Remediation\r\nCommitment:\r\n*Teachers commit to strictly implementing scheduled remediation sessions within or beyond class hours. 5. Learner Grouping and Differentiated Instruction\r\nCommitment:\r\n*Teachers commit to applying differentiated instruction and flexible grouping strategies to address diverse learner needs.\r\n*Teachers commit to promoting peer tutoring and collaborative learning to strengthen mastery of competencies.        6. Strong School Leadership and Support\r\nCommitment (School Heads and Teachers):\r\n*School heads commit to providing continuous instructional supervision, monitoring, and technical assistance to ensure effective remediation implementation.\r\n*Teachers commit to actively cooperating with school leadership initiatives and complying with recommended interventions.                7. Collaboration Among Teachers\r\nCommitment:\r\n*Teachers commit to actively participating in LAC sessions and collaborative planning for remediation programs.\r\n*Teachers commit to sharing best practices, strategies, and materials to improve intervention effectiveness.                 8. Monitoring and Feedback Mechanisms\r\nCommitment:\r\n*Teachers commit to regularly monitoring learner progress and maintaining accurate records of remediation outcomes.\r\n*Teachers commit to providing timely and constructive feedback and adjusting strategies to ensure continuous learner improvement.', '1. Strong Teacher Values Orientation and Commitment:\r\n*Teachers commit to consistently model the DepEd Core Values (Maka-Diyos, Maka-tao, Makakalikasan, Makabansa) in daily interactions inside and outside the classroom.\r\n*Teachers commit to serving as positive role models of respectful, ethical, and responsible behavior that learners can emulate.                             2. Integration of Values in Lesson Planning Commitment:\r\n*Teachers commit to intentionally integrating peace education and DepEd Core Values in all lesson plans and daily lessons.\r\n*Teachers commit to embedding values in learning activities, discussion questions, and performance tasks to ensure holistic learner development.                 3. Effective Classroom Management Practices\r\nCommitment:\r\n*Teachers commit to consistently applying positive and restorative discipline strategies that promote respect and responsibility.\r\n*Teachers commit to maintaining a safe, inclusive, and respectful classroom environment conducive to values formation and learning.                               4. Use of Contextualized and Relevant Materials\r\nCommitment:\r\n*Teachers commit to developing and utilizing contextualized learning materials that reflect local culture, values, and real-life situations.  5. Supportive School Culture and Leadership\r\nCommitment:\r\n*Teachers commit to actively participating in school-wide values formation programs such as Peace Month activities and flag ceremonies.\r\n*Teachers commit to supporting and implementing school leadership initiatives that promote values integration across all learning areas.          6. Collaboration Among Teachers\r\nCommitment:\r\n*Teachers commit to actively sharing values-integrated lesson exemplars and strategies during LAC sessions and professional learning communities.\r\n*Teachers commit to collaboratively planning and implementing activities that promote peace education and core values integration. 7. Community and Stakeholder Support\r\nCommitment:\r\n*Teachers commit to engaging parents and community stakeholders in reinforcing DepEd Core Values and peace education initiatives.\r\n*Teachers commit to strengthening home-school-community partnerships that support values formation among learners.                             8. Monitoring and Reinforcement Mechanisms\r\nCommitment:\r\n*Teachers commit to participating in regular classroom observations and feedback sessions focusing on values integration practices.\r\n*Teachers commit to continuously improving instruction based on feedback and recognition systems that highlight effective values education practices.', '1. Technical Knowledge and Skills Gaps Issues:\r\n*Limited understanding of item analysis concepts.\r\n*Difficulty interpreting results.\r\n*Lack of skills in using tools (e.g., Excel)\r\nProposed Resolutions:\r\na. Capacity-Building and Training\r\n*Include actual computation and interpretation exercises\r\nb. Development of Simplified Tools\r\n*Provide ready-to-use Excel templates with automated computation.\r\nc. Coaching and Technical Assistance\r\n*Assign master teachers or TIA coordinators to mentor others.\r\n*Conduct follow-up coaching sessions after assessments\r\n2. Time Constraints and Workload\r\nIssue:\r\n*Limited time for conducting TIA\r\nProposed Resolutions:\r\na. Scheduling and Time Allocation\r\n*Integrate TIA into the school assessment calendar.\r\n*Allocate specific days for:\r\nChecking\r\nItem analysis\r\nPlanning interventions.\r\nb. Streamlining Processes\r\n*Use automated tools/templates to reduce computation time.\r\nc. Workload Sharing\r\n*Encourage team-based item analysis per grade level/subject\r\n*Distribute tasks (data encoding, analysis, reporting)\r\n3. Data Utilization Gaps Issues:\r\n*TIA results not used for improvement\r\n*Lack of action plans\r\n Proposed Resolutions:\r\na. Data-Driven Instructional Planning\r\n*Require teachers to identify least mastered competencies (LMCs) and develop targeted remediation plans.\r\nb. Action Planning Integration\r\n*Include TIA results in Lesson planning (DLL/DLP),\r\nremediation and enrichment programs.\r\nc. Tracking and Documentation\r\n*Maintain TIA reports with corresponding interventions and outcomes.\r\n*Monitor progress of learners after interventions.\r\n4. Collaboration and Professional Support Issues:\r\n*Limited discussion in LAC.\r\n*Lack of peer support\r\n Proposed Resolutions:\r\na. Strengthening LAC Sessions\r\n*Dedicate LAC meetings to \r\nanalyzing TIA results and \r\nsharing best practices.\r\nb. Collaborative Analysis\r\n*Conduct group-based TIA workshops per subject area.\r\n*Encourage peer mentoring and coaching.\r\nc. Professional Learning Communities (PLC)\r\n*Establish teams that regularly review onTest construction,\r\nAnalysis results and \r\nInstructional adjustments.\r\n5. Monitoring and Feedback Gaps Issue:\r\n*Lack of feedback on TIA quality\r\nProposed Resolutions:\r\na. Instructional Supervision\r\n*School heads conduct regular review of TIA outputs.\r\n*Provide constructive feedback on \r\nitem quality,\r\naccuracy of analysis and\r\nalignment with competencies\r\nb. Quality Assurance Mechanism\r\n*Require submission and review of TIA reports after major tests.\r\nc. Recognition and Support\r\n*Recognize teachers with accurate and effective use of TIA.\r\n*Provide targeted support for those needing improvement.\r\n6. Attitude and Practice Issues:\r\n*Perception that TIA is a burden\r\nProposed Resolutions:\r\na. Awareness and Advocacy\r\n*Orient teachers on the importance and benefits of TIA in improving learning outcomes.\r\nb. Simplification of Processes\r\n', 'The indicator pertains to the TVL track of Senior High School and is not aligned with elementary offerings.', '1. Strong Implementation of Child Protection Policy (CPP)\r\nCommitment:\r\n*The school commits to strictly enforce the Child Protection Policy through a fully functional Child Protection Committee (CPC). 2. Teacher Awareness and Proactive Classroom Management\r\nCommitment:\r\n*Teachers commit to maintaining vigilance in identifying and addressing early signs of bullying or conflict.\r\n*Teachers commit to immediately intervene and apply positive discipline strategies to prevent escalation of incidents.                     3. Values Education and Peace Promotion\r\nCommitment:\r\n*Teachers commit to consistently integrating respect, empathy, kindness, and inclusivity in daily lessons across all learning areas.    4. Awareness Campaigns and School Programs\r\nCommitment:\r\n*The school commits to regularly conduct child protection and anti-bullying orientations for learners, teachers, and parents                     5. Active Parent and Community Involvement\r\nCommitment:\r\n*The school commits to actively engage parents in child protection initiatives through orientations, meetings, and communication platforms.\r\n*The school commits to strengthening partnerships with community stakeholders to ensure a safe and supportive environment for learners both in and out of school.            6. Monitoring and Reporting System\r\nCommitment:\r\n*The school commits to ensure immediate documentation, investigation, and resolution of any reported concern, maintaining confidentiality and child protection standards.', '1. Strong Implementation of Child Protection Policy (CPP)\r\nCommitment:      *The school commits to strictly enforce the Child Protection Policy in accordance with DepEd guidelines at all times.                                2. Preventive Education and Values Formation\r\nCommitment:\r\n*Teachers commit to integrating child rights, personal safety, and respect in all learning areas and classroom activities.                              3. Safe and Supportive School Environment\r\nCommitment:\r\n*The school commits to maintaining a child-friendly, inclusive, and respectful learning environment where all learners feel safe and protected.              4. Active Parent and Community Involvement\r\nCommitment:\r\n*The school commits to  orient parents on child protection policies and positive parenting practices.\r\n*The school commits to strengthening partnerships with barangay officials, social workers, and community stakeholders to ensure a protective environment for learners beyond the school.                                   5. Effective Monitoring and Reporting Mechanisms\r\nCommitment:\r\n*The school commits to maintaining functional, accessible, and confidential reporting systems for any child protection concerns.\r\n', '1. Strong Student Monitoring System\r\nCommitment:\r\n*The school commits to implement a continuous monitoring system for attendance, academic performance, and student behavior, for all identified at-risk learners.              2. Positive and Safe School Environment\r\nCommitment:\r\n*The school commits to maintain a safe, inclusive, and child-friendly environment, enforcing anti-bullying policies and promoting respect, well-being, and student protection at all times.\r\n3. Active Parental and Community Involvement\r\nCommitment:\r\n*The school commits to strengthen partnerships with parents and community stakeholders through regular communication and meetings.                         4. Flexible Learning Options\r\nCommitment:\r\n*The school commits to provide inclusive and adaptable learning modalities (e.g., modular, blended, or alternative delivery modes) to address the diverse needs and circumstances of learners.\r\n5. Recognition and Motivation Systems\r\nCommitment:\r\nThe school commits to  sustain recognition programs that celebrate student achievements, improvements, and positive behaviors to encourage continued engagement and persistence.', '1. Limited Cultural Awareness and Training\r\nIssue: Teachers and staff lack sufficient knowledge on culturally responsive practices.\r\nProposed Resolutions:\r\n*Integrate cultural responsiveness topics into Learning Action Cell (LAC) sessions or in-service trainings.\r\n*Partner with local cultural experts, elders, or community leaders to provide authentic insights and guidance.                              2. Limited Student Participation\r\nIssue: Students feel shy, excluded, or uncomfortable participating.\r\nProposed Resolutions:\r\n*Create a safe and inclusive environment where all cultures are respected and valued.\r\n*Use participatory and student-centered activities (e.g., group work, storytelling, peer sharing) to encourage involvement.. 3.Budget Constraints\r\nIssue: Insufficient funding limits activities.\r\nProposed Resolutions:\r\n*Allocate a specific budget for culture-sensitive programs in the School Improvement Plan (SIP).                         *Utilize low-cost or indigenous materials and community resources instead of expensive items.\r\n*Seek partnerships and sponsorships from local government units (LGUs), NGOs, and community organizations.\r\n*Organize fundraising activities (e.g., search/candidates) to support programs.', '1. Stigma and Low Self-Confidence Among Learners\r\nIssue: Learners feel embarrassed or fear judgment.\r\nProposed Resolutions:\r\n*Conduct orientation and advocacy sessions emphasizing that education is open and inclusive for all ages.                                 *Highlight success stories of OSYs and adult learners to inspire confidence.  2. Time Constraints and Scheduling Conflicts\r\nIssue: Schedules do not match learners’ availability.\r\nProposed Resolutions:\r\n*Offer flexible learning schedules (e.g., weekend, or modular learning options).\r\n*Implement self-paced or blended learning approaches to accommodate working learners and family duties.        3. Weak Stakeholder and Community Support\r\nIssue: Limited involvement of community leaders and partners.\r\nProposed Resolutions:\r\n*Strengthen partnerships with local government units (LGUs), NGOs, and community leaders.                 *Engage barangay officials and local in identifying and encouraging potential learners.', 'Proposed Resolutions to Address Low ALS Enrollment\r\n1. Strengthen Community Mapping and Targeting\r\n*Conduct a house-to-house survey with barangay officials to identify out-of-school youth and adults (OSYA).\r\n&Use existing records (e.g., dropouts, LIS data) to build a master list of potential learners.                               2. Intensify Advocacy and Awareness Campaigns\r\n*Launch localized information drives (posters, social media, barangay announcements). *Integrate ALS promotion during school events, PTA meetings, and community gatherings.                          3. Strengthen Partnerships with Community Stakeholders\r\nCollaborate with barangay officials, SK leaders, NGOs, and religious groups for referrals.                      4. Integrate ALS in School Improvement Planning\r\nInclude ALS enrollment strategies in the SIP/AIP.\r\n*Allocate budget specifically for advocacy and program implementation.', '1. Designated CPC members with specific tasks.\r\nProposed Commitment:\r\n*The school commits to maintaining a fully staffed Child Protection Committee.                  2. Orientation of members on roles and responsibilities to ensure accountability\r\nProposed Commitment:\r\n*The school commits to conducting orientation for all CPC members at the start of school year.                                       3. Teachers, parents, students, and community partners are engaged and aware of child protection initiatives.\r\nProposed Commitment:\r\n*The school commits to sustaining active stakeholder engagement through continuous awareness campaigns, regular communication, and inclusive participation in child protection programs and activities.                            4. Time, budget, and materials are allocated to sustain the committee’s programs and activities\r\nProposed Commitment:\r\n*The school commits to allocating adequate and sustained resources—including  time, budget, and materials—within the annual school plan to ensure uninterrupted implementation of child protection initiatives.', '1. Established DRRM committee with clear roles\r\nProposed Commitment:\r\n*The school commits to maintaining a  functional DRRM committee with clearly defined roles, updated annually, and supported by official designations and terms of reference to ensure continuity and accountability.                     2. Conduct of regular drills and simulations\r\nProposed Commitment:\r\n*The school commits to conducting regular and well-documented disaster drills (e.g., earthquake, fire, evacuation) at least quarterly, with post-drill evaluations to continuously improve response effectiveness.3. Integration into school programs and curriculum\r\nProposed Commitment:\r\nThe school commits to continuously integrating DRRM concepts into classroom instruction, co-curricular activities, and school events to reinforce awareness and preparedness among all learners.\r\n4. Capacity building and training\r\nProposed Commitment:\r\n*The school commits to provide training during LAC session  for teachers and  staff, and symposium / training  for students on disaster preparedness, first aid, and emergency response protocols. 5. Strong coordination with local authorities and partners\r\nProposed Commitment:\r\n*The school commits to sustaining active partnerships with local DRRM offices, health units, and emergency responders through regular coordination, joint activities, and information sharing.', '1. Stigma and reluctance to seek help\r\nProposed Resolution:\r\n*Implement sustained mental health awareness and advocacy campaigns (e.g., orientations, classroom discussions, peer support programs, information drives) to normalize help-seeking behavior, reduce stigma, and promote a supportive school environment.\r\n2. Limited trained personnel\r\nProposed Resolution:\r\n*Strengthen capacity by providing training for selected teachers and staff on basic psychosocial support, mental health first aid, and referral mechanisms; establish linkages with external professionals (e.g., counselors, psychologists, local health units) to supplement in-school support.', '1. Absence of accessible infrastructure\r\nProposed Resolution:\r\n*Develop a phased accessibility improvement plan that includes construction of standard ramps, accessible toilets, and safe pathways, prioritized in the School Improvement Plan. 2. Lack of SPED-specific facilities and learning spaces\r\nProposed Resolution:\r\n*Allocate a designated SPED learning space within the school and gradually equip it with appropriate learning materials, assistive devices, and adaptive resources to support diverse learner needs.                3. Lack of trained personnel to support SPED learners\r\nProposed Resolution:\r\n*Provide continuous professional development for teachers through training on inclusive education and SPED handling, and designate focal teachers while building partnerships with SPED specialists or external experts.\r\n4. Insufficient funding for facility improvement\r\nProposed Resolution:\r\n*Integrate SPED and accessibility needs into annual budgeting, strengthen proposal writing for external funding (e.g., LGU, NGOs, donors), and build partnerships to support infrastructure and resource development', '1.Strengthen data collection system. *Assign a data focal person .     *Establish  timeline for submission and validation of NAT related-data. 2.Capacity building *Train teachers on data recording, analysis and interpretation.  *Use digital  tools like utilizing spread sheet (Excel, Google Sheets) for accurate tracking .     3.Fexible participation options           *Schedule activities at convenient times or allow both physical and virtual participation. 4.Resource mobilization *Conduct fund raising activities (e.g Brigada Eskwela, donation drives and school search / candidate *Strengthen school-community partnerships   like collaborating barangay officials, LGU, NGOs, alumni and private sector for support.', '1.Flexible and inclusive scheduling *Schedule meetings based on members, availability.          *Offer hybrid options(face-to-face or online meetings). 2.Integration with existing activities meetings   *Combine related activities to save time.                        3.Build strong partnerships *Engaging barangay officials, alumni, local businesses, LGU, NGOs. *Organize fund raising activities like donation drives, search/ candidate. *Develop project proposal to LGU for funding. \r\n*Request assistance from LGU, DepEd, NGOs.          4.Prioritize urgent facility and ICT needs                                 .* Implement improvements gradually based on available resources.', '1.Strengthening Representation  *Introducing pre-election leadership screening, including interviews, leadership portfolios, or teacher recommendations.\r\n*Conduct candidate orientations about their roles and responsibilities. *Promote issue-based campaigns rather than popularity-based campaigning. 2.Improving Communication Channels    *Establish official student feedback systems, such as suggestion boxes (physical and digital).               * Utilize social media platforms and bulletin boards for transparent updates and announcements. 3.Leadership and Skills Development\r\n*Organize leadership training programs for all SSG/SPG officers.\r\n*Partner with teachers and external speakers to enhance skills in communication and project management. 4.Increasing Student Participation \r\n*Design student-centered and interest-based activities to encourage voluntary participation.\r\n*Introduce incentive systems, such as recognition, certificates, or merit points.\r\n5.Monitoring and Evaluation l\r\n*Conduct quarterly evaluations of SSG/SPG performance and student satisfaction .\r\n*Submit reports to the school administration for review and support.', '1. Lack of Customer Service Training for Frontline Personnel *Conduct customer service training workshops focusing on communication skills, professionalism, and responsiveness. *Establish a feedback system (surveys, suggestion boxes, online forms) to evaluate staff performance and stakeholder satisfaction.                    2. Poor Record-Keeping Systems (Risk of Lost or Incomplete Records)                *Assign a Records Management Officer for organization.      *Provide training on proper records handling and data management for staff.           *Transition to a digitized records management system (scanned and encoded files). *Standardize filing systems, document formats, and naming conventions. 3.Implementation Support Measures *Partner with ICT experts or local stakeholders for technical assistance.', '1. Inconsistent monitoring and follow-through\r\nProposed Resolution:\r\n*Establish a structured monitoring and evaluation (M&E) system with a fixed schedule (e.g., monthly reviews), clear performance indicators, and assigned focal persons responsible for tracking, reporting, and follow-up actions.          2. Limited alignment between plans and actual practice\r\nProposed Resolution:\r\n*Conduct regular alignment and review sessions to translate implementation plans into specific, time-bound tasks; ensure all staff understand their roles and integrate planned activities into their daily/weekly work plans.                                    3. Resource constraints (budget, materials, manpower)\r\nProposed Resolution:\r\n*Integrate implementation plan requirements into the school’s annual budgeting and resource planning; prioritize key activities, mobilize additional support from stakeholders/partners, and optimize available resources through proper allocation.                            4. External constraints and interruptions\r\nProposed Resolution:\r\nDevelop contingency and flexible implementation strategies (e.g., rescheduling mechanisms, alternative delivery modes, backup timelines) to ensure continuity of activities despite disruptions such as class suspensions or community issues.', '1. Irregular or absent SGC meetings\r\nProposed Resolution:\r\n*Establish a fixed meeting schedule (e.g., quarterly or monthly) approved at the start of the school year.                      2. Lack of clear roles and responsibilities\r\nProposed Resolution:\r\n*Develop and disseminate clear role descriptions and terms of reference for all SGC members, followed by an orientation to ensure understanding of duties, expectations, and accountability mechanisms.                   3. Weak stakeholder engagement\r\nProposed Resolution:\r\n*Strengthen stakeholder participation by creating inclusive activities, flexible meeting schedules, and regular communication (e.g., invitations, updates, feedback channels) to encourage active involvement of parents and community partners.                           4. Limited awareness of SGC purpose and importance\r\nProposed Resolution:\r\n*Conduct continuous awareness and advocacy efforts (e.g., orientations, assemblies, information materials) to clearly communicate the role, benefits, and impact of the SGC in improving school governance and outcomes.', '1. Inconsistent attendance and participation of parents\r\nProposed Resolution:\r\n*Strengthen parent engagement through regular communication (group chats, letters), flexible meeting schedules, and diverse participation modes (online/offline), while recognizing active parent involvement to encourage sustained participation.                    2. Scheduling conflicts among members\r\nProposed Resolution:\r\n*Adopt flexible scheduling of PTA meetings (e.g., multiple time options, weekend/after-work sessions) and set meeting calendars at the start of the school year to allow parents to plan ahead.                              3. Uneven engagement of PTA officers and members\r\nProposed Resolution:\r\n*Clearly define roles and responsibilities of PTA officers, establish accountability mechanisms, and conduct regular performance check-ins to ensure balanced participation and shared responsibility.\r\n4. Resource limitations for PTA activities\r\nProposed Resolution:\r\n*Develop a sustainable resource mobilization plan through partnerships, fundraising activities, and collaboration with LGUs and stakeholders, while ensuring transparent and efficient use of PTA funds.                                    5. Limited training or orientation for PTA officers\r\nProposed Resolution:\r\n*Conduct regular orientation and capacity-building sessions for PTA officers on leadership, governance, financial management, and school partnership roles to strengthen effectiveness.', '1. Regular coordination and communication\r\nProposed Commitment:\r\n*The school commits to maintaining continuous and structured communication with stakeholders and partner schools through regular meetings, consultations, and digital platforms to ensure sustained collaboration and coordination.\r\n2. Culture of collaboration and openness\r\nProposed Commitment:\r\n*The school commits to nurturing a culture of collaboration, trust, and openness by actively engaging stakeholders in school programs, decision-making processes, and joint activities that strengthen partnerships.                  3. Continuous monitoring and evaluation of partnerships\r\nProposed Commitment:\r\n*The school commits to regularly monitoring and evaluating all partnership activities to assess effectiveness, address gaps, and continuously improve collaborative initiatives.\r\n4. Recognition and sustainability of partnerships\r\nProposed Commitment:\r\n*The school commits to recognizing and appreciating partner contributions through formal acknowledgment (e.g., certificates, awards, and public recognition) and sustaining successful partnerships through continuous engagement and renewal of agreements.', '1. Data Collection & Management\r\nIssue: Incomplete, inconsistent, delayed reports\r\nProposed Resolutions:\r\n*Standardize reporting using uniform templates and clear guidelines (with sample accomplished forms)\r\n*Shift to digital submission systems (e.g., shared drives or simple tracking sheets) with auto-deadline reminders\r\n*Set fixed reporting schedules (e.g., weekly/monthly cut-offs) and enforce them consistently.                                 2. Capacity & Skills of Personnel\r\nIssues: Limited M&E training; weak data analysis skills\r\nProposed Resolutions:\r\n*Conduct regular capacity-building workshops on basic M&E, data interpretation, and reporting                       *Use peer mentoring or coaching systems (pair skilled staff with those needing support)                               3. Time Constraints & Workload\r\nIssue: Overloaded teachers and staff\r\nProposed Resolutions:\r\n*Integrate M&E tasks into regular teaching and administrative workflows (not as an extra task). *Distribute responsibilities through team-based monitoring, not individual burden.                                4. Communication & Coordination\r\nIssue: Minimal stakeholder involvement\r\nProposed Resolutions:\r\n*Establish regular stakeholder meetings (PTA, community forums, student consultations)\r\n*Use multiple communication channels (social media groups, SMS, bulletin boards). *Involve stakeholders in planning, implementation, and evaluation, not just reporting                         5. Resource Constraints\r\nIssue: Insufficient budget\r\nProposed Resolutions:\r\n*Integrate M&E costs into the annual school improvement plan (SIP) and budget planning.              *Prioritize low-cost or free digital tools (e.g., spreadsheets, free survey.                     * Track and demonstrate results to justify increased funding supportplatforms)', 'Strengthen instructional leadership by providing regular coaching, mentoring, and performance feedback to all personnel, Implement regular monitoring and evaluation to track progress and provide timely interventions when needed, Recognize and reward exemplary performance to motivate personnel and sustain high levels of achievement', 'School leaders will maintain active monitoring, provide clear direction, and ensure that all performance indicators are consistently met, School leaders will maintain active monitoring, provide clear direction, and ensure that all performance indicators are consistently met, Strengthen Collaboration and Teamwork', 'Strengthen Instructional Leadership Support, Enhance Teacher Participation and Collaboration, Continuously Evaluate and Improve Programs', 'Actively disseminate available trainings, seminars, and scholarships and ensure fair and equal participation among personnel, Sustain Continuous Improvement Practices', 'Promote Fair and Transparent Selection Processes, •  Sustain a Positive and Motivating School Culture, Regularly review and enhance recognition policies and practices based on feedback and emerging needs.', 'Maintain Accurate and Updated Personnel Records, Ensure Timely Submission of Required Documents, Strengthen Administrative Capacity', 'Ensure strict adherence to DepEd guidelines and established standards in assigning teaching loads and ancillary tasks, Enhance Participatory Planning and Consultation, School management shall continuously monitor workload assignments to ensure fairness, efficiency, and compliance with standards', '1. Use of Standardized Tools\r\nCommitments:\r\n*The school commits to regularly updating and using standardized inspection checklists and reporting forms for all facilities.\r\n*The school will implement and maintain digital tracking systems (e.g., logs or spreadsheets) for real-time monitoring of issues.                                    2. Responsive Maintenance System\r\nCommitments:\r\n*The school commits to establishing a clear, accessible reporting mechanism (e.g., suggestion box, or digital form) for defects and hazards.                                *A feedback system will inform stakeholders about the status and resolution of reported concerns.    3. Adequate Resources and Budget\r\nCommitments:\r\n*The school commits to allocating a dedicated annual budget for maintenance and minor repairs.\r\n*Funds will be regularly reviewed and adjusted based on emerging needs and priorities. *Transparent financial reporting on maintenance expenditures will be conducted periodically.                     4. Stakeholder Participation\r\nCommitments:\r\n*The school commits to actively encouraging teachers and students to report hazards through regular orientations and reminders.                    *A school-wide awareness campaign will promote shared responsibility for maintaining facilities.                *Feedback from stakeholders will be documented and integrated into maintenance planning                              5. Leadership and Accountability\r\nCommitments:\r\n&The school head commits to conducting regular monitoring and walkthrough inspections (e.g., monthly).\r\n*A designated Physical Facilities Coordinator will oversee all maintenance-related activities. *Roles and responsibilities will be clearly defined and communicated to all involved personnel.\r\n*Performance related to facilities maintenance will be included in staff accountability and evaluation systems.', '1. Funding Instability and Limitations Proposed Resolutions:\r\n*Establish a dedicated maintenance and repair fund with clear allocation for preventive and emergency needs.\r\n*Implement annual and multi-year infrastructure planning, prioritizing projects based on urgency and impact.\r\n*Strengthen financial monitoring and transparency (regular budget reviews, expense tracking, reporting).\r\n*Mobilize additional funding sources such as partnerships, donations, and community support initiatives.                           2. Stakeholder Engagement Issues \r\nProposed Resolutions:\r\n*Conduct regular orientations and awareness campaigns highlighting the importance of shared responsibility in maintaining school facilities. *Strengthen communication channels (meetings, group chats, bulletin boards) to keep stakeholders informed and involved.  *Recognize participation through giving of certificates.  *Involve stakeholders in planning and decision-making, not just implementation, to build ownership.', '1.Start with a Low-Cost / Incremental Library Setup\r\n*Begin with reading corner in the classroom.                                         2. Allocate and Institutionalize a Library Budget\r\n*Integrate library development into the School Improvement Plan (SIP) and Annual Implementation Plan (AIP).   *Establish a phased funding approach (e.g., Year 1: space + basic books; Year 2: expansion + furniture; Year 3: digital resources).', '1. Intermittent Utility Supply (Unstable Water Supply)\r\nProposed Resolutions:\r\n*Install or upgrade a water storage system (e.g., overhead tanks or reserve tanks) to ensure continuous supply during low pressure.                             2. Aging or Inadequate Infrastructure (Old Electrical Wirings)\r\nProposed Resolutions: *Implement a comprehensive electrical rewiring and upgrading program based on safety standards.           3. Budget Constraints (for Rewiring and Water Improvement)\r\nProposed Resolutions:\r\n*Integrate utility upgrades into the School Improvement Plan (SIP) and Annual Budget Plan for sustained funding. *Strengthen partnerships with LGUs, NGOs, and private stakeholders for funding support. *Conduct resource mobilization activities (donation drives and fund raising).              *Prioritize phased implementation (urgent repairs first, followed by full system upgrades).\r\n*Ensure transparent financial tracking to build trust and attract more support.', '1. Absence of ICT Infrastructure (No Computer Laboratory / ICT Room)\r\nProposed Resolutions:\r\n*Designate and convert an existing classroom or multipurpose room into a functional ICT corner/lab.\r\n*Implement a phased ICT facility development plan (start small, expand gradually).         *Include ICT space development in the School Improvement Plan (SIP) and Annual Implementation Plan (AIP) for continuous upgrading.                         2. Lack of Essential Equipment (Computers, Laptops, Printers, Networking Devices)\r\nProposed Resolutions:              *Integrate ICT procurement into annual budget planning with clear prioritization. *Procure minimum functional ICT sets (even 3–5 computers to start) for shared student use.\r\n*Mobilize support through donations from alumni, LGUs, NGOs, and private partners.                          3. Support Systems to Sustain ICT Functionality\r\nProposed Resolutions:\r\n*Assign a teacher or ICT focal person to manage and monitor equipment use.                        *Establish simple usage rules and schedules to ensure proper utilization.', '1. Transparent and Regular Financial Reporting\r\n*The school commits to monthly and quarterly financial reporting of MOOE utilization to ensure continuous monitoring.\r\n*All financial reports will be accurate, timely, and accessible to authorized stakeholders for transparency.                 2. Complete and Organized Documentation\r\n*The school commits to maintaining complete financial records including receipts, liquidation reports, vouchers, and summaries.             3. Strict Compliance with Financial Policies\r\nThe school commits to full compliance with DepEd accounting and auditing rules and regulations.                     4. Needs-Based Budget Allocation\r\n*The school commits to allocating funds strictly based on identified school needs such as maintenance, utilities, and learning resources. *Priority will be given to urgent and high-impact expenditures that directly support learning continuity. 5. Participatory and Data-Driven Planning\r\n*The school commits to involving key stakeholders (teachers, coordinators, and administrators) in budget planning.\r\n*Financial decisions will be based on validated data and documented school needs.', '1. Commitment to Full Compliance with Government Rules and Regulations\r\n*The school commits to strict adherence to COA and DepEd financial rules, guidelines, and prescribed deadlines at all times.                          *The school will ensure that all personnel involved in finance are continuously updated on new policies and issuances.                          2. Commitment to Strengthened Organizational Discipline and Culture of Compliance\r\n*The school commits to maintaining a strong culture of transparency, accountability, and responsibility in financial management.                       *All staff involved in MOOE utilization will be oriented that liquidation is mandatory, time-bound, and non-negotiable.                     3. Commitment to Complete and Timely Documentation\r\n*The school commits to ensuring that all supporting documents (receipts, invoices, vouchers, liquidation reports) are complete for every transaction.', '1. Communication & Transparency\r\nIssue: Important announcements not reaching all stakeholders on time\r\nProposed Resolutions\r\n*Establish a clear communication protocol.\r\n*Use a multi-channel approach\r\nso no stakeholder is left out.\r\n*Create and maintain an updated stakeholder database.\r\n*Set standard timelines for announcements.\r\n*Assign a designated communication focal person/team. *Require acknowledgment or confirmation system.\r\n*Conduct regular monitoring of communication effectiveness.', 0, 110, 12, 57);
