(2712, 'Learner and Staff Mental Health and Well Program', '1.Promote learner and staff mental health through structured wellness initiatives.\r\n2. Provide accessible counseling and peer support mechanisms.\r\n3. Strengthen collaboration with health professionals to sustain mental wellness programs.', '1. Counseling records documenting sessions and intervention\r\n2. Wellness activity reports (seminars, workshops, group sessions).\r\n3. Peer support group records showing participation and outcomes.\r\n4. Consolidated quarterly monitoring reports for SBM validation.', '1. Conduct quarterly wellness sessions (stress management, mindfulness, resilience training).\r\n2. Provide individual and group counseling sessions for learners and staff.\r\n3. Establish peer support groups to encourage positive relationships and shared coping strategies.\r\n4. Partner with health professionals (psychologist, nurses, social workers) for technical support.\r\n5. Document activities and share best practices during LAC and SBM review meetings.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n', 'Quarterly', 'Guidance Designate, Teachers', '300.00', 'Partner with health professionals to ensure sustainability and credibility of wellness initiatives. Continues monitoring and reporting will strengthen mental health support for learners and staff.', 2026, 129041, 59, 12, 147),
(2713, 'Sustaining a Safe, Inclusive, and Child-Friendly Learning Environment', 'To maintain and continuously improve the physical and psychosocial learning environment of Little Baguio ES', '•	Well-maintained classrooms and school facilities\r\n•	Implemented child protection and disaster preparedness measures\r\n•	Monitoring and safety compliance reports\r\n', 'Maintenance activities, stakeholder support, regular safety inspections', 'Throughout the School Year', 'School Head, Teachers, Parents, Learners and ', '100000', '', 2026, 128999, 60, 12, 29),
(2714, 'Participatory Planning and Transparent Utilization of School Resources', 'To ensure effective, transparent, and needs-based use of financial and material resources', '•	Implemented Annual Implementation Plan (AIP)\r\n•	Financial and liquidation reports\r\n•	Updated resource inventory\r\n', 'Collaborative planning, financial monitoring, documentation and reporting', 'July 2026 – March 2027', 'School Head, Property Custodian, Teachers, SG', '2000', '', 2026, 128999, 60, 12, 29),
(2715, 'Activity 1:  Empowering the School Governing Council (SGC) ', 'To form a representative and functional School Governing Council (SGC) comprising diverse stakeholders.\r\nTo clearly define the roles, responsibilities, and decision-making authority of the SGC.\r\nTo enhance the capacity of SGC members through targeted', 'A formally constituted SGC with elected or appointed members representing parents, teachers, students, community members, and local education authorities.\r\nA signed constitution or by-laws outlining the SGC\'s operational framework, meeting schedules, and decision-making processes.\r\nA comprehensive training program completed by SGC members, evidenced by certificates or attendance records.\r\nEvidence of the SGC actively participating in key school decisions (e.g., budget allocation, policy development).', 'takeholder Identification and Nomination: Identify all relevant stakeholder groups and establish a transparent nomination and election process for SGC membership.\r\nSGC Constitution Development: Draft and ratify a constitution for the SGC, ensuring it aligns with national/regional SBM guidelines.\r\nCapacity Building Workshops: Design and deliver training modules covering topics such as leadership, school finance, legal frameworks, strategic planning, and conflict resolution.\r\nRegular SGC Meetings: Facilitate regular meetings of the SGC to discuss school performance, review plans, make decisions, and monitor progress.', '2nd Quarter of the School Year particularly A', 'School Head and SGC committee', '2000.00', 'To fully operationalize the functionality of SGC', 2026, 129027, 59, 12, 147),
(2716, 'Strengthening Partnerships with Parents and Community Stakeholders', 'To deepen stakeholder engagement and shared accountability in achieving school goals', '•	Sustained stakeholder participation\r\n•	MOAs or documented support from partners\r\n•	Stakeholder-assisted school programs\r\n', 'Networking, partnership meetings, advocacy and collaboration activities', 'Throughout the School Year', 'School Head, Teachers, Parents, LGU Represent', '2000', '', 2026, 128999, 60, 12, 29),
(2718, '6. FINANCE AND RESOURCE MANAGEMENT AND MOBILIZATION\r\n-resource generation and financial management program', 'to ensure efficient use and generation of school resources', '-increased school funds/resources\r\n-proper financial reports\r\nstrong stakeholder partnerships', '-fundraising activities\r\n-partnership with stakeholders\r\n-transparent budgeting', 'June 2026- March 2027', 'School Head, Finance Officer, PTA, LGU', '5000.00', 'SUBJECT TO AUDITING AND LIQUIDATION', 2026, 304297, 59, 12, 151),
(2719, 'Activity 2: Developing a More Functional School Improvement Plan (SIP) through Participatory Processes', 'o conduct a thorough needs assessment of the school, identifying areas for improvement in academic, infrastructural, and operational aspects.\r\nTo engage all stakeholders in a collaborative process to define the school\'s vision, mission, and strategic', 'A detailed School Needs Assessment Report, including data on student performance, resource availability, teacher capacity, and community feedback.\r\nA clearly articulated School Vision and Mission statement, agreed upon by stakeholders.\r\nA finalized School Improvement Plan (SIP) with specific, measurable, achievable, relevant, and time-bound (SMART) objectives, key strategies, activities, responsible persons, required resources, and performance indicators.\r\nDocumented evidence of stakeholder participation in SIP development (e.g., meeting minutes, feedback forms).', 'Data Collection for Needs Assessment: Utilize surveys, interviews, focus groups, and analysis of school records (academic results, attendance, financial reports) to gather comprehensive data.\r\nStakeholder Visioning Workshops: Conduct workshops where stakeholders collaboratively define the school\'s future direction, aspirations, and core values.\r\nSIP Drafting Committee: Form a committee, including SGC members and school management, to draft the SIP based on assessment findings and visioning outcomes.\r\nCommunity Review and Feedback: Present the draft SIP to the wider school community for review, feedback, and final approval.\r\nAction Planning: Break down SIP goals into specific, manageable action steps with clear timelines and assigned responsibilities.', '2nd quarter of the year , April to June', 'School Head, SGC, SPTA, Learner Representativ', '3000.00', '', 2026, 129027, 59, 12, 147),
(2720, 'Monitoring, Evaluation, and School-Based Review of Performance Indicators', 'To monitor progress of SBM implementation and use results for continuous improvement', '•	SBM monitoring and evaluation reports\r\n•	Identified best practices and areas for improvement\r\n•	Updated action plans based on findings\r\n', 'Data analysis, review meetings, feedback mechanisms', 'Semi-Annually / Quarterly', 'School Head, SBM Coordinator, Teachers, SGC M', '4000', '', 2026, 128999, 60, 12, 29),
(2721, 'Conduct Self-Assessment', 'To conduct the SBM Self-Assessment', 'Conducted SBM Self-Assessment', 'Filling out Self-Assessment Form', 'April 2026', 'School Heads, Teachers, Stakeholders', '500', 'Conduct SBM Self-Assessment', 2026, 129180, 61, 12, 3),
(2722, 'Activity 3: Enhancing Financial Management and Resource Mobilization Transparency', 'To establish transparent and accountable financial management systems at the school level.\r\nTo improve the efficiency and effectiveness of resource allocation based on the SIP priorities.\r\nTo explore and implement diverse strategies for mobilizing ad', 'A clear and accessible school budget, approved by the SGC and communicated to stakeholders.\r\nRegular financial reports detailing income, expenditure, and budget variance, made publicly available.\r\nImplemented internal control mechanisms to ensure proper use of funds and prevent mismanagement.\r\nEstablished partnerships or initiatives for resource generation (e.g., fundraising events, grant applications, community contributions).\r\nIncreased stakeholder confidence in the school\'s financial management practices.', 'Budgeting Process: Develop an annual school budget in alignment with the SIP, involving input from department heads and approval by the SGC.\r\nFinancial Record Keeping: Implement a robust system for tracking all financial transactions, ensuring accuracy and completeness.\r\nInternal Controls: Establish procedures such as dual signatories for checks, procurement guidelines, and regular audits (internal or external).\r\nResource Mobilization Strategy: Identify potential funding sources (government grants, private sector partnerships, alumni donations, community fundraising) and develop specific strategies to pursue them.\r\nPublic Disclosure: Regularly publish financial statements and budget implementation reports on the school notice board, website, or through community meetings.', 'April to June of the Year', 'School Head, AO, Teachers, BAC', '1000.00', '', 2026, 129027, 59, 12, 147),
(2723, 'Integrate the results of the SBM Self-Assessment in the AIP', 'To integrate the SBM results in the AIP', 'Integrated SBM Self-Assessment results in the AIP', 'Integration of SBM', 'June 2026', 'School Heads, Teachers, Stakeholders', '500', 'Integration of Self-Assessment in the AIP', 2026, 129180, 61, 12, 3),
(2724, 'Conduct Culture-Sensitive Activities', 'Promote respect for diversity and conduct activities that reflect local culture and traditions', '* Conducted cultural events/celebrations\r\n* Integrated cultural content in lessons\r\n* Documentation of activities', '* Identify local cultural practices and traditions\r\n* Integrate culture-based content in lesson plans\r\n* Organize School cultural festival or exhibits\r\n* Invite community elders/leaders as resource speakers\r\n* Display cultural artifacts or information boards', '1st. to 4th Quarter', 'ALS Coordinator, teachers LGU, School head', '2000', 'Update records quarterly and report to the Division Office regularly...', 2026, 128915, 59, 12, 142),
(2725, ' Capacity Building Workshops', 'To train teachers, staff, and stakeholders on SBM principles.', 'Increased awareness and skills in participatory decision-making', 'Conduct seminars, training sessions, peer learning and mentoring.', 'June 2026', 'School Head, Teachers, Parents', '500.00', '', 2026, 128923, 59, 12, 143),
(2726, 'Recognition of Exemplary Performance (MALBULEN )', 'To recognize outstanding teachers and stakeholders who contribute to SBM implementation and school improvement.', '•	Recognized awardees\r\n•	Program documentation\r\n•	Increased motivation and performance\r\n', '•	Implementation of MALBULEN Awards\r\n•	Recognition and awarding ceremony\r\n', 'March 2027', 'School Head, PRAISE Committee, Teachers, Stak', '40000', '', 2026, 128999, 60, 12, 29),
(2727, 'Accessibility Enhancement Program', 'Ensure inclusivity for learners with disabilities.', 'Accessibility audit reports; learner feedback.', '1. Upgrade facilities for accessibility.\r\n2. Provide assistive devices and materials.', 'Annual review', 'SNED Teachers, School Head, LGU', '5000.00', 'Align with inclusive education standards.', 2026, 129041, 59, 12, 147),
(2728, 'I-PLANNING AND DECISION MAKING\r\nII-COMMUNITY PARTICIPATION\r\nIII-CURRICULUM AND INSTRUCTION\r\nIV-RESOURCE MANAGEMENT\r\nV-MONITORING AND EVALUATION', '1.To collaboratively develop and implement effective school plans through data driven decision making, active stakeholders participation and alignment with school goals to improve learner outcomes.\r\n2.To actively engage parents ,community members and', '1. IMPROVE LEARNERS ACHIEVEMENT  AND ACADEMIC PERFORMANCE\r\n2. FUNCTIONAL AND UPDATED SCHOOL IMPROVEMENT PLAN\r\n3. ACTIVE AND SUSTAINED STAKEHOLDERS PARTICIPATION\r\n4.EFFICIENT AND TRANSPARENT USE OF SCHOOL RESOURCES\r\n5.STRENGTHEN SCHOOL GOVERNING COUNCIL\r\n6.REGULAR MONITORING AND EVALUATION REPORTS ON SCHOOL PROGRAM\r\n7.IMPROVE TEACHING LEARNING PROCESSES AND LEARNER CENTERED INSTRUCTION.', '1. 1. Conduct regular PTA and school governing council meeting.\r\n2. Engage parents and community in school programs and decision making.\r\n3. Build partnerships with LGU\'s and private organizations.\r\n4. Use learners performance data to guide intervention.\r\n5. Conduct regular assessment and school profiling.\r\n6. Based SIP or AIP activities on identified needs.\r\n7. Applied learners centered and differentiated instructions.\r\n8. Conduct remediation and enrichment programs.\r\n', 'YEAR- ROUND', 'School Head, Master Teachers,Teachers and ICT', '400000.00', 'The school demonstrates a level 3 SBM practice , indicating an advanced and mature level of implementation characterized by strong shared governance active stakeholders participation and well-established system for continuous improvement.', 2026, 129157, 61, 12, 1),
(2729, 'Activity 4: Diagnostic Assessment of Reading and Numeracy Skills', 'To accurately identify the current reading and numeracy proficiency levels of all students in the target grade(s).\r\nTo pinpoint specific areas of difficulty for individual students and groups (e.g., phonics, comprehension, basic arithmetic operations', 'Individual learner diagnostic reports detailing strengths and weaknesses in reading (e.g., decoding, fluency, vocabulary, comprehension) and numeracy (e.g., number sense, calculation, geometry, data analysis).\r\nClassroom-level and school-level summary reports highlighting common challenges and learning gaps.\r\nA categorized list of students requiring targeted support, differentiated by skill level.', 'Standardized Assessment Tools: Select and administer age-appropriate, standardized diagnostic assessments for reading and numeracy (e.g., phonics screeners, reading comprehension tests, math concept inventories).\r\nTeacher-Administered Observations: Train teachers to conduct informal diagnostic observations during regular classroom activities, noting student engagement and problem-solving strategies.\r\nData Analysis and Interpretation: Systematically analyze assessment results to identify patterns and specific learning needs.\r\nData Triangulation: Cross-reference results from different assessment methods to ensure validity and a comprehensive understanding of each student\'s profile.', 'June, 2026', 'School Head, School Reading and Numeracy Foca', '0.00', 'To alleviate the reading and numeracy skills of the learners especially those who are with poor numeracy and reading skills', 2026, 129027, 59, 12, 147),
(2730, 'Activity 6: Comprehensive Customer Feedback System Implementation', 'o establish multiple, accessible channels for customers to provide feedback on services.\r\nTo systematically collect, analyze, and categorize customer feedback to identify key satisfaction drivers and areas for improvement.\r\nTo ensure that customer fe', 'A functional multi-channel feedback system (e.g., online surveys, suggestion boxes, direct feedback forms, post-service interviews).\r\nRegularly generated reports summarizing customer feedback trends, common issues, and satisfaction scores (e.g., Net Promoter Score - NPS, Customer Satisfaction Score - CSAT).\r\nA database of customer feedback categorized by service type, issue, or department.\r\nEvidence of feedback being reviewed by management and integrated into service improvemen', 'Channel Selection and Setup: Identify the most appropriate feedback channels based on customer demographics and service delivery points. Set up and promote these channels.\r\nFeedback Form Design: Develop clear, concise, and user-friendly feedback forms that capture relevant information without being overly burdensome.\r\nData Collection and Aggregation: Implement a process for consistently collecting feedback from all channels and aggregating it into a central system.\r\nQualitative and Quantitative Analysis: Analyze feedback using both quantitative methods (e.g., scoring) and qualitative methods (e.g., thematic analysis of comments) to understand the \'what\' and the \'why\' behind customer sentiment.', 'April to June of the Year', 'ICT experts, AO, School Head and Teachers', '2000.00', 'for efficient tracking of customer feedback', 2026, 129027, 59, 12, 147),
(2731, 'Implement plans for continuous improvement of the plan', 'To craft and implement a plan ', 'Implemented plans for continuous improvement', 'Implementation of the plan', 'October 2026-March 2027', 'School Head, Teachers, Stakeholders', '500', 'Implemented', 2026, 129180, 61, 12, 3),
(2732, 'HAPPY CORNER FOR EVERY READER', 'TO PROVIDE READER WITH POSITIVE AND UPLIFTING SPACE WHERE THEY CAN FIND INSPIRATION, RELAXATION AND SIMPLE MOMENTS OF JOY, ENCOURIGING A HEALTHIER MINDSET, EMOTIONAL WELL-BEING AND A BRIGHTER OUTLOOK ON EVERYDAY LIFE.', 'HAPPY CORNER FOR EVERY READER REMAINS ENGAGING, INCLUSIVE AND CONSISTENLY UPLIFTING, HELPING EVERY READER EXPERIENCE MOMENTS OF POSITIVE AND RELAXATION', '1. CONTENT SELECTION\r\n2. ORGANIZATION OF MATERIALS\r\n3.ACCESSIBILITY\r\n4. READER ENGAGEMENT\r\n5. INTERACTIVE ELEMENTS', 'JUNE- DECEMBER 2026', 'TEACHERS AND SCHOOL HEAD', '22844', '', 2026, 205053, 60, 12, 29),
