(4463, 'WINS program', 'Upgrading PWD friendly facilities and WASH Stations', 'functional WASH stations and PWD friendly facilities', 'Repair of WASH stations ', 'June 2026', 'school head, property custodian, SPTA', '', '', 2026, 304405, 108, 12, 45),
(4464, 'SBM Self-assessment', 'To strengthen the School-Community Planning Team (SPT) and transparency in school operations', 'Sustain the Advanced (Level 3) SBM maturity', 'Conduct of quarterly SBM  MOVs organization', 'quarterly', 'school head, SBM focal person', '', '', 2026, 304405, 108, 12, 45),
(4465, 'Conduct orientation of SBM for teachers, parents and community stakeholders', '1. to strengthen community governance by empowering stakeholders. 2. to collaborate improve  learning outcome, resource management and accountabilities. ', 'Increased awareness and understanding of SBM principles among stakeholders\r\nStrengthened collaboration between school and community\r\nActive participation of stakeholders in school programs and decision-making\r\nImproved plans for learning outcomes, resource management, and accountability\r\nDocumented attendance and orientation materials (photos, reports, minutes)', 'Conduct face-to-face orientation session using presentations (PowerPoint/visual aids)\r\nInteractive discussions and open forum to address concerns and clarify roles\r\nGroup workshops to identify stakeholder contributions and responsibilities\r\nDistribution of SBM guides, handouts, or brochures\r\nSharing of best practices and success stories in SBM implementation', 'Preparation: 1–2 weeks before the activity Or', 'School Head / Principal (Activity Lead) Teach', '20000', 'Ensure alignment with DepEd SBM policies and guidelines\r\nEncourage maximum participation from all stakeholder groups\r\nDocument the activity thoroughly for reporting and future reference\r\nFollow up with action plans or next steps after the orientatio', 2026, 325106, 103, 12, 0),
(4466, 'Implementation of School-Based Management (SBM) Program', 'To strengthen participatory governance, improve school performance, and ensure continuous school improvement through active stakeholder involvement', 'Functional SBM system, improved school programs, active stakeholder participation, updated school plans, and better learner outcomes', 'Conduct orientation on SBM, organize SBM Committee, review and update SIP, mobilize resources, implement programs/projects, monitor accomplishments, and evaluate results through regular meetings and consultations', 'June 2026 – March 2027', 'School Head, Teachers, Non-Teaching Personnel', '15000', 'Materials Needed: bond paper, printer ink, folders, pens, notebooks, tarpaulin, certificates, snacks, cleaning materials, documentation materials, and other office supplies. Subject to availability of funds and stakeholder support.', 2026, 129402, 103, 12, 126),
(4467, '\"Conduct of School Personnel Orientation on Policy Updates and Strengthening SBM Governance Mechanisms\"', 'a. To conduct a comprehensive reorientation of teaching and non-teaching personnel on the enhanced guidelines of the Revised School-Based Management (SBM) System to ensure administrative compliance and operational excellence.\r\nb. To formalize the est', '\"Established and operationalized SBM Pillar Committees with clearly defined roles and responsibilities to manage school governance and operations.\"', 'Gather teachers before the start of the School Year', 'May 2026', 'School Head', '2000', 'To be implemented within the time frame of Brigada Eskwela\r\nImplementation\r\n', 2026, 129001, 60, 12, 29),
(4468, '\"Stakeholder Engagement Orientation and Capability-Building for the Operationalization of SBM Pillar Committees\"', 'a. To mobilize internal and external stakeholders—including the School Governance Council (SGC), PTA, and LGU partners—by clarifying their distinct roles, responsibilities, and accountabilities in achieving improved learning outcomes.', 'To establish and functional SBM Pillar Committees with clearly defined duties and responsibilities.', 'Signed Commitment Wall or Pledge of Commitment\r\n\r\nOrganizational Chart of SBM Teams\r\n', 'April- May 2026', 'School Head and Administrative Officer II', '2000', 'To be implemented within the period of implementation of Brigada Eskwela', 2026, 129001, 60, 12, 29),
(4469, '\"SBM Self-Assessment & Benchmarking\"', 'Infrastructure Priority Assessment: \r\na.	To conduct a comprehensive audit of the Physical Plant and Facilities to determine the magnitude of required rehabilitation and prioritize improvement areas based on urgency, safety, and impact on learning.\r\nD', '-	Infrastructure Audit\r\nA prioritized list of facilities for repair/rehab based on a 5-point priority scale.\r\n\r\n-	Data Consolidation\r\nA validated School Profile containing essential performance and resource data.\r\n\r\n-	SWOT Analysis\r\nA strategic roadmap identifying the vision-reality gap and priority root causes', 'Physical Walkthrough: Conduct a room-by-room audit using a standardized Facility Inspection Checklist.\r\n\r\nPrioritization Matrix: Categorize findings into \"Immediate Rehabilitation\" (safety hazards) vs. \"Scheduled Maintenance\" (aesthetic or minor repairs).\r\n\r\nCondition Rating: Score each facility (e.g., 1–5 scale) based on safety, functionality, and instructional impact.\r\n', 'May 2026- June 2026', 'School Head, Teaching and Non-Teaching Person', '1500', 'Conducted after the teacher\'s break and during Brigada Eskwela to capture both internal and external stakeholders while they are already engaged in school preparations.', 2026, 129001, 60, 12, 29),
(4470, '\"Creation of the\r\nSchool-Based\r\nManagement\"\r\nCenter', 'a.	To establish a centralized repository and showcase of school performance data, governance documents, and evidence of continuous improvement.\r\nb.	To provide a dedicated workspace for SBM Pillar Committees to collaborate, monitor projects, and facil', 'A fully functional SBM Center/Hub (Physical or Virtual) organized according to the four pillars of the Revised SBM System.\r\n\r\n• Structured Document Management System (E-copy and Hard copy) containing the \"Means of Verification\" (MOVs) for SBM validation.\r\n\r\n• Standardized Visual Displays (Dashboards) of the School Report Card (SRC) and Continuous Improvement (CI) projects.', '• Standardization & Categorization: Use a \"Pillar-Based Filing System\" where documents are color-coded and tagged according to Leadership, Curriculum, Accountability, and Resource Management.\r\n• Digital Integration: Implement a QR-code-based filing system for paperless access to school records and real-time data monitoring.\r\n• Collaborative Setup: Organize the center as a \"Working Hub\" rather than just a storage room, equipped with meeting facilities for the SGC and SBM Teams.', 'June 2026 March 2028', 'School Head, Teaching and non-teaching Person', '10000', '• Sustainability: The center should be updated monthly, not just during validation season, to reflect real-time school progress.\r\n• Accessibility: Ensure the center is located in a space accessible to both internal staff and external stakeholders (SGC/PTA) to promote transparency.\r\n• Policy Compliance: Layout and documentation must strictly follow the requirements of DepEd Order No. 007, s. 2024 to ensure it passes Division-level validation.', 2026, 129001, 60, 12, 29),
(4471, '\"Resource\r\nMobilization\r\nManagement\"', '• To diversify school funding sources and material support through the formalization of partnerships with local government units (LGUs), NGOs, and private sector stakeholders.\r\n• To institutionalize a transparent and accountable system for the utiliz', '• Resource Mobilization Plan: A strategic roadmap identifying resource gaps and potential donor targets.\r\n\r\n• Signed Memoranda of Agreement (MOA/MOU): Formalized legal partnerships with external stakeholders\r\n\r\n• Resource Tracking Dashboard: A transparent registry of donations, grants, and labor (Brigada Eskwela) with corresponding utilization reports.', '• Stakeholder Mapping & Needs Matching: Identifying the specific strengths of partners (e.g., LGU for infrastructure, NGOs for literacy) and matching them with school needs.\r\n• Advocacy & Proposal Pitching: Developing professional project proposals to be presented to potential donors during \"Stakeholders\' Day\" or community assemblies\r\n• Transparency Mechanism: Implementing a \"Transparency Board\" or digital registry where all donations and expenditures are published for public viewing.', 'June 2026 October 2026', 'School Head, Teaching and Non-Teaching Person', '2500', '• Policy Adherence: All donations must be processed through the Adopt-A-School Program (ASP) guidelines to ensure tax incentives for donors and proper government accounting.\r\n• Equity: Ensure that mobilized resources are distributed fairly across different grade levels and learning centers based on need, not just popularity.\r\n• Sustainability: Focus on long-term partnerships rather than one-time donations to ensure the continuity of school programs.', 2026, 129001, 60, 12, 29),
(4472, '\"Strategic School Planning and Development\"', 'a.	To formulate a three-year School Improvement Plan (SIP) and an Annual Implementation Plan (AIP) that are data-driven and responsive to the school’s priority improvement areas.\r\nb.	To synchronize school programs and projects with DepEd’s MATATAG Ag', 'a.	Approved School Improvement Plan (SIP): The strategic roadmap for the next three school years.\r\n\r\nb.	Annual Implementation Plan (AIP): The year-specific operational plan with corresponding budget allocations.\r\n\r\nc.	Project Designs & Monitoring Tools: Specific action plans for each pillar committee (e.g., feeding programs, reading interventions).', 'a.	Participatory Planning Workshop: Facilitating break-out sessions where each SBM Pillar committee drafts their specific targets based on the Situation Analysis.\r\nb.	Target Setting & Forecasting: Using historical enrollment and performance data to set S.M.A.R.T. (Specific, Measurable, Achievable, Relevant, Time-bound) objectives.\r\nc.	Quality Assurance Review: Conducting a \"Internal Peer Audit\" where different teams critique each other\'s plans for feasibility before finalization. ', 'May 2026- July 2026', 'School Head, Teaching and Non-Teaching Person', '2000', 'Effective school planning should be data-driven, participatory, and responsive to learners’ needs. Continuous review and adjustment of plans ensure relevance and alignment with school performance goals.', 2026, 129001, 60, 12, 29);
INSERT INTO `sgod_action_plan` (`id`, `activity`, `objective`, `ex_output`, `metho_strategy`, `time_frame`, `person_involved`, `bud_req`, `remarks`, `fy`, `school_id`, `division`, `region`, `district`) VALUES
(4473, '\"Implementation of Programs, Projects, and Activities (PPAs)\"', 'a.	To execute targeted interventions and school-based programs designed to address specific Priority Improvement Areas (PIAs).\r\nb.	To ensure that all activities are implemented in strict accordance with the approved Annual Implementation Plan (AIP) a', 'a.	Accomplished Activity Designs: Fully executed programs with documented results and evidence of completion.\r\nb.	PPA Progress Reports: Monthly or quarterly summaries of physical and financial accomplishments.\r\nc.	Service Delivery Milestones: Tangible improvements in school KPIs (e.g., increased literacy levels, repaired facilities, or completed teacher trainings).', 'a.	Project Management Cycle: Applying the \"Initiation-Planning-Execution-Closing\" framework for every major school activity to ensure organization.\r\nb.	Delegation & Empowerment: Assigning \"Project Owners\" for each PPA, giving committee heads the authority and accountability to lead their specific activities.\r\nc.	Resource Synchronization: Coordinating the release of MOOE funds and donated materials to match the specific timelines of each project.', 'May 2026 March 2027', 'School Head and Administrative Officer II', '10000', '• Budget Realism: Ensure that the planning is not just a \"wish list\" but is grounded in the available MOOE and Mobilized Resources.\r\n• Inclusivity: The planning team MUST include a student representative and a community leader (LGU/PTA) to comply with the School Governance Council (SGC) mandate.\r\n• Flexibility: The plan should be a \"living document\" capable of being adjusted during the Mid-Year Review based on emerging needs.', 2026, 129001, 60, 12, 29),
(4475, '\"Institutionalization of the School Monitoring, Evaluation, and Adjustment (SMEA) System\"', 'a.	To establish a systematic tracking mechanism for the implementation of the Annual Implementation Plan (AIP) and individual project progress.\r\nb.	To identify bottlenecks and operational gaps in real-time to allow for immediate management interventi', '• Quarterly SMEA Reports: Documented findings on physical and financial accomplishments versus targets.\r\n\r\n• SBM Dashboards: Visual trackers showing the status of each pillar’s Key Performance Indicators (KPIs).\r\n\r\n• Adjusted Implementation Plans: Revised strategies or \"catch-up\" plans based on identified delays or challenges.', '• Data-Driven Review (DDR): Conducting quarterly SMEA conferences where pillar coordinators present their evidence of progress and undergo peer validation.\r\n• Field Observation & Validation: Moving beyond paper reports by conducting \"spot checks\" and classroom walkthroughs to verify if activities are happening as planned.\r\n• Process Monitoring: Using standardized M&E templates (e.g., PMCF - Progress Monitoring and Coaching Form) to document technical assistance provided to teachers and staff.', 'May 2026 March 2027', 'School Head, Teaching and Non-Teaching Person', '5000', '• Non-Punitive Approach: M&E should be viewed as a tool for support, not for finding fault. The goal is \"Adjustment,\" not just \"Monitoring.\"\r\n• Evidence-Based: All \"accomplishments\" must be backed by the SBM Center’s Means of Verification (MOVs).\r\n• Stakeholder Feedback: Include surveys or feedback loops from parents and students to evaluate the quality of school services from the \"customer\" perspective.', 2026, 129001, 60, 12, 29),
(4476, '\"School Documentation and Reporting\"', 'a.	To establish a standardized system for the collection, verification, and archiving of Means of Verification (MOVs) across all SBM pillars.\r\nb.	To ensure the timely and accurate submission of mandatory school reports (SMEA, SRC, e-SIP) to the Distr', 'a.	SBM Evidence Portfolios: Organized digital and physical folders containing narratives, photos, and documents for validation.\r\nb.	Annual School Report Card (SRC): A simplified, visual summary of school performance shared with the community.\r\nc.	Narrative Accomplishment Reports: Detailed accounts of programs and projects, including \"Lessons Learned\" and \"Best Practices.\"', '-	Real-Time Documentation: Implementing a \"Document-As-You-Go\" policy where committee members upload photos and attendance sheets to a shared cloud drive immediately after every activity.\r\n-	Quality Assurance (QA) Desk: Designating a lead documenter to review all reports for consistency, grammar, and alignment with DepEd formatting standards.\r\n-	Storytelling through Data: Using infographics and \"Success Stories\" to make technical reports more engaging for parents and external partners.', 'May 2026 March 2027', 'School Head, Teaching and Non-Teaching Person', '10000', '• Data Privacy: Ensure that all documentation, especially photos and student data, strictly complies with the Data Privacy Act of 2012.\r\n• Verification: Every report must be signed by the relevant Pillar Coordinator and the School Head to ensure official accountability.', 2026, 129001, 60, 12, 29),
(4477, '\"School Rewards, Recognition, and Feedback Mechanism\"\r\n(Rewarding Outstanding \r\nGenuinely \r\nEducators, and\r\nRole-model Stakeholders)', 'a.	To establish a formal Rewards and Recognition (R&R) System to incentivize outstanding performance among teaching and non-teaching personnel.\r\nb.	To create a structured Feedback Loop for students, parents, and partners to evaluate school services a', 'a.	School R&R Policy Guidelines: A customized manual for selecting and awarding \"Best Performing\" staff and stakeholders.\r\n\r\nb.	Quarterly Recognition Rites: Organized ceremonies (physical or virtual) to honor achievers and partners.\r\n\r\nc.	Consolidated Feedback Report: A summary of stakeholder suggestions and complaints with corresponding \"Management Actions\" taken.', '-	Merit-Based Evaluation: Utilizing the Results-Based Performance Management System (RPMS) and SBM targets as the basis for staff awards.\r\n-	Multi-Channel Feedback: Implementing \"Suggestion Boxes,\" digital survey forms (Google Forms), and \"Stakeholder Forums\" to gather diverse perspectives.\r\n-	Public Commendation: Leveraging social media and the School Report Card (SRC) to highlight the contributions of LGU and private partners.', 'May 2026 March 2027', 'School Head, Teaching and Non-Teaching Person', '15000', '• Fairness & Objectivity: The criteria for awards must be clear, documented, and communicated to everyone before the evaluation period starts.\r\n• Actionable Feedback: Feedback is useless if not acted upon; every major concern raised by stakeholders should be addressed in the next SMEA adjustment.\r\n• Inclusivity: Ensure that \"Small Wins\" (e.g., perfect attendance, timely report submission) are recognized alongside major achievements to maintain high staff morale.', 2026, 129001, 60, 12, 29),
(4478, '“Institutionalization of the Continuous Improvement (CI) Program and Sustainability”', 'a.	To identify and eliminate recurring operational bottlenecks through the application of Continuous Improvement (CI) methodologies (e.g., Plan-Do-Check-Act).\r\nb.	To ensure the longevity of school programs by integrating them into the Annual Implemen', 'a.	Continuous Improvement Project Reports: Documented \"Before and After\" results of specific interventions (e.g., Reading Recovery, Waste Management).\r\n\r\nb.	Operations Manual/SOPs: Standardized guidelines for successful programs to ensure they continue even after leadership or staff changes.\r\n\r\nc.	Sustainability Roadmap: A long-term resource and succession plan to maintain school maturity levels.', '• The \"Small Wins\" Approach: Launching pilot CI projects that target specific, manageable problems with immediate impact on learner performance.\r\n• Knowledge Management (KM): Creating a digital repository of \"Best Practices\" and \"Lessons Learned\" to prevent the loss of institutional knowledge during staff transitions.\r\n•Regular Benchmarking: Conducting exchange visits or peer learning sessions with other \"Level 3\" SBM schools to adapt successful governance models.', 'May 2026 March 2027', 'School Head, Teaching and Non-Teaching Person', '5000', '• Ownership: Sustainability is achieved when stakeholders feel they \"own\" the project; avoid top-down mandates in favor of collaborative problem-solving.\r\n• Resource Resilience: Avoid over-reliance on a single donor; develop a \"Resource Diversification\" strategy to ensure funding stability.\r\n• Integration: Ensure that CI projects are not \"extra work\" but are embedded into the daily teaching-learning and administrative processes.', 2026, 129001, 60, 12, 29),
